      <workList xml:id="workDesc_e0a8483b">
         <work xml:id="work_idf4f3d00c">
            <identifier label="SchW" xml:id="identifier_3b7a84db">C4:013</identifier>
            <title xml:id="title_id267439e1" xml:lang="de">Søren Knudsens Heldengeschichte oder eine gelehrte Streitschrift von den Zweykämpfen</title>
            <title type="original" xml:id="title_fee6e513" xml:lang="da">Søren Knudsens Helden-Geschichte, eller: En Disputatz om Dueller</title>
            <contributor xml:id="respStmt_6bec7a22">
               <persName role="author" xml:id="persName_f76a0345">Søren Bloch</persName>
               <persName role="translator" xml:id="persName_idb08a02b8">Johann Adolph Scheibe</persName>
            </contributor>
            <creation xml:id="creation_id7c89ecb2">
               <date isodate="1756" xml:id="date_c3338209">1756</date>
            </creation>
            <history xml:id="history_id810d0cd7">
               <p xml:id="idm120">One of the last books which Scheibe translated while living in Sønderborg was <persName xml:id="idm121" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Søren Bloch</persName>’s satirical essay, <rend xml:id="idm122" fontstyle="italic">Søren Knudsens Heldengeschichte</rend> of 1755, which mocks the duelling so popular in the eighteenth century. Soon after its Danish publication, it appeared in a German translation issued by <persName xml:id="idm123" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Franz Christian Mumme</persName> with whom Scheibe had worked in connection with other translations. In March 1756, an extensive review of the translation appeared in <rend xml:id="idm124" fontstyle="italic">Efterretninger om Nye Bøger og Lærde Sager</rend>, in particular criticising aspects of translating a satirical work: thus Scheibe had for instance a tendency in his translation to revise the text avoiding the offensive language which the author used. Apparently Scheibe seems to have been aware of that for the son of the author remarked later that 'immediately afterwards the royal Kapellmeister Scheibe complained about the translation published by Mumme’s Bookshop in 1756; the witty original work was, however, too much obscured by Danicisms' (1784-translation, sig. A2r). Presumably for this reason, a new translation was issued in 1784 at the son's instigation. Though the book of 1756 does not indicate the name of the translator, the work is listed in Scheibe's short biography which appeared in <rend xml:id="idm125" fontstyle="italic">Fortgesetzte Nachrichten</rend> of 1759.</p>
            </history>
